Obtaining a copy
----------------

IEEE 2030.5 is available at no charge through the IEEE GET Program:

    https://standards.ieee.org/products-programs/ieee-get-program/

Accept IEEE's terms and download the standard. Both artifacts are distributed
with the standard's XML artifacts (the XML/xmlspy model release), where they
appear as sep_wadl.xml and sep.xsd. Your use of those files is governed by
IEEE's terms, not by this project's license.

This module drives its conformance sweep from one specific document: IEEE
2030.5-2018, Model Build 20180301, sep_wadl.xml. See test/conformance/README.md
for its identity, the digest the tests verify, and how to point the build at a
copy you have obtained.


Pointing the build at your copy
-------------------------------

Either place the file at the conventional location, which is gitignored so it
cannot be committed by accident:

    test/conformance/wadl/sep_wadl.xml

or set an environment variable to any path you like:

    export SEP2_WADL_PATH=/path/to/sep_wadl.xml

SEP2_WADL_PATH wins over the conventional location. Then:

    go test ./test/conformance/...

Without a copy, the WADL-gated tests SKIP and the rest of the suite runs
normally, so a contributor who has not obtained the standard still gets a
green build. The skip is reported by go test as a skip, never as a pass, and
each skipped test names SEP2_WADL_PATH and this file.

A skip is not a conformance verdict. To require that a WADL really was found,
so that a missing or misconfigured copy fails the run instead of quietly
skipping the gate, set:

    export SEP2_WADL_REQUIRED=1

Continuous integration that supplies the WADL should set it. With it set, an
absent WADL is a hard failure; the value must be a boolean that Go's
strconv.ParseBool accepts, and anything else is itself an error, so a typo
cannot silently disarm the gate.


The schema (sep.xsd)
--------------------

The schema gate is implemented in the core module, ieee-2030_5-core-go, in
packages schema and internal/xsdgate, and is configured there by
SEP2_SCHEMA_PATH and SEP2_SCHEMA_REQUIRED. Its NOTICE covers the schema's
attribution. The contract is identical in shape to the WADL contract described
above, deliberately, so that one convention covers both artifacts. See
test/conformance/README.md.
